@page "/settings"
@using Odyssey.ApiClient.Contracts
@using Odyssey.ApiClient.Resources
@using Odyssey.Client.Authorization
@using Odyssey.Dtos.Authorization
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = PermissionClaims.SystemSettingsRead)]
@inject Odyssey.Client.Services.IPageStateService PageState
@inject ISystemSettingsApiClient SystemSettings
@inject ISecretSettingsApiClient SecretSettings
@inject Odyssey.ApiClient.Resources.IDataExportApiClient DataExport
@inject Odyssey.Client.Services.IImportLimitsCache ImportLimits
@inject Odyssey.Client.Services.IUploadLimitsCache UploadLimits
@inject Odyssey.Client.Services.IAccountLimitsCache AccountLimits
@inject Odyssey.Client.Services.IFileAnalysisDisclosureCache Disclosures
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject ISnackbar Snackbar
@inject AuthenticationStateProvider Auth
@* The browser's own origin, for the client-side origin-mismatch hint on the client base URL row
   (issue #8 §3.3) — the server cannot see it. *@
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>System settings</PageTitle>

@*
    System settings — the admin-only page for workspace-wide configuration
    (Odyssey Design System · SystemSettings, issue #349). Distinct from
    Preferences, which is per-user: these controls govern the whole Odyssey
    instance, so the page lives in the admin nav section and is gated by
    system-settings.read.

    The card is the GROUP, not the setting: each labelled group is one section card
    holding a grid of OdsSettingField blocks (issue #343, extended since with the
    per-surface caps, the file-analysis runtime rows and the Subscriptions limits).
    At 40+ settings the earlier one-card-per-setting stack of OdsSettingRows was a run
    of near-identical shells whose visual weight said nothing about the content; the
    grouping now does the work those card borders were doing. Sections and rows both
    come from the Sections catalogue in the code-behind — never hand-listed here, which
    is what kept the old count in this comment wrong.

    The write claim is split by AXIS, not by group: system-settings.security.update for
    the Security toggles AND the import/export-size (MB) caps — the availability knob —
    and system-settings.update for everything else, enforced per row via Editable(),
    never a single page-level flag, so a caller lacking one claim still edits the fields
    it holds the other for. The PUT payload sends null — not the loaded value — for every
    field the caller cannot edit; that null contract is what lets the split claim coexist
    with a whole-resource PUT with no concurrency token (see SystemSettingsService on the
    API side). A count cap is tri-state — a finite number or explicitly "No limit"
    (OdsCapacityField) — and each import/export group carries a cross-field round-trip
    rule (export cap ≤ import cap) rendered as a group-level alert, since the offending
    row may itself be disabled (unlimited).
*@

<MudStack Spacing="6">

    @* The setup signal (issue #445, extended by #8). An unreadable credential is an OUTAGE, not a
       settings problem: the value is stored and its consumer is failing right now, and neither the
       cause nor the fix is a Save. So it goes in the header's severity rollup — the same gesture
       Accounts, Insurance and Tax statements use for a data condition needing attention — and
       deliberately NOT into BlockingProblems, which exists to explain a disabled Save button. Merging
       them would make Save look blocked by something Save cannot fix.

       Issue #8 put a second KIND of entry in the same rollup: mail that has never been configured, at
       Information rather than Error. That is why the label is "Attention" and no longer "Credentials"
       — a rollup headed by the wrong noun is worse than a generic one, and this now covers both a
       credential that cannot be decrypted and a deployment that has not finished being set up. It is
       the label TaxStatementsCard already uses for the same mixed-severity shape. *@
    <PageHeader Title="System settings" Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Settings"
                Problems="@SetupProblems"
                ProblemsLabel="Attention"
                SearchOpen="_searchOpen" SearchOpenChanged="OnSearchToggled">
        <SubLine>Instance-wide configuration for this Odyssey deployment</SubLine>
        <PrimaryAction>
            @if (CanSave)
            {
                @* The save bar: the primary action, and — when Save is blocked — the ErrorSummary
                   that names the count and moves focus to the offending control. The catalogue runs
                   to 40+ rows across seventeen sections, so a disabled Save with its cause two
                   screens away is an unexplained dead end.

                   Deliberately NOT a live region: ErrorFor recomputes on every keystroke, so a
                   summary inside one would interrupt a screen-reader user once per character. The
                   same text is announced politely on a Save ATTEMPT instead (see Save()). *@
                <div class="ss-savebar">
                    @if (_phase == Phase.Ready && BlockingProblems.Count > 0)
                    {
                        <OdsErrorSummary Problems="@BlockingProblems" OnJump="JumpToProblem" />
                    }
                    <OdsButton Icon="@(_justSaved ? "check" : "save")"
                               Loading="_isSaving"
                               Badge="@PendingBadge"
                               BadgeLabel="unsaved changes"
                               Disabled="@(_phase != Phase.Ready || HasErrors)"
                               OnClick="Save">
                        @(_justSaved ? "Saved" : "Save changes")
                    </OdsButton>
                </div>
            }
        </PrimaryAction>
        <SearchContent>
            <OdsSearchField Value="@_search" ValueChanged="OnSearchChanged"
                            Placeholder="Search settings…" />
        </SearchContent>
    </PageHeader>

    @* Single most-recent-change summary — the store is genuinely key-value server-side, but the DTO
       surfaces only the max UpdatedAt/UpdatedBy across every row (a deliberate v1 simplification),
       so this renders once for the page rather than per row. *@
    @if (_phase == Phase.Ready && _dto is { UpdatedBy: not null })
    {
        <div class="ss-lastchanged">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.History" Size="Size.Small" />
            Last changed by <b>@(_dto.UpdatedByDisplayName ?? "Unknown user")</b> on @_dto.UpdatedAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("d MMM yyyy, HH:mm")
        </div>
    }

    @if (_phase == Phase.Ready && !CanSave)
    {
        <div class="ss-note" role="note">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Lock" />
            <div>
                <div class="ss-note-ttl">You have read-only access to system settings</div>
                <div class="ss-note-desc">These values are visible to you but can only be changed by an administrator with the matching update permission.</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    @* The persistent, non-live record of any row not using its stored value (issue #437 Goal 12).

       A PAGE-LEVEL block with its OWN condition, deliberately not nested in either neighbouring
       banner's @if. BlockingSummary sits inside @if (CanSave), so placing this beside it would give a
       caller holding only system-settings.read no record at all — the users least able to repair the
       rows and most likely to be auditing them — and would also make a fault and an unsaved edit
       mutually exclusive, since that is an if/else-if chain. ss-note is the opposite gate (!CanSave),
       so reusing its condition produces the same hole with the sign flipped. The claim-blind neighbour
       is ss-lastchanged, whose shape this follows.

       It is also outside the VisibleSections branch: the sections container is replaced by the empty
       state exactly when the search matches nothing, which is the case this block most needs to survive.

       A page-owned element, not an Ods* wrapper carrying Class="ss-faultsummary": a scoped rule keyed
       on a class forwarded into a child component never matches. *@
    @if (_phase == Phase.Ready && FaultSummary is { } faultSummary)
    {
        <div class="ss-faults" role="note">
            <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">report_problem</span>
            <div class="ss-faults-body">@faultSummary</div>
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Text" Size="Size.Small" OnClick="GoToFirstFault">
                Go to first fault
            </MudButton>
        </div>
    }

    @if (_phase == Phase.Loading)
    {
        @* Field-shaped blocks in the same grid the loaded page uses, so nothing reflows when the
           real rows arrive. *@
        <div class="ss-sect-stack" aria-busy="true" aria-label="Loading settings">
            @foreach (var sec in Sections)
            {
                <section class="ss-sect">
                    <header class="ss-sect-h">
                        <span class="ss-sect-ic"><MudIcon Icon="@sec.Icon" Size="Size.Small" /></span>
                        <span class="ss-sect-t" style="opacity: 0.6">@sec.Group</span>
                    </header>
                    <div class="ss-sect-body">
                        <div class="odc-sfield-grid">
                            @for (var i = 0; i < sec.Items.Count; i++)
                            {
                                <div class="odc-sfield ss-sk" aria-hidden="true">
                                    <div class="odc-sfield-frame">
                                        <span class="ss-sk-val"></span>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="odc-sfield-help"><span class="ss-sk-line"></span></div>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>
            }
        </div>
    }
    else if (_phase == Phase.Error)
    {
        <div class="ss-retry" role="alert">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.CloudOff" />
            <div class="ss-retry-ttl">Couldn't load settings</div>
            <div class="ss-retry-desc">Something went wrong reading the system settings. Your changes weren't affected.</div>
            <OdsButton Variant="OdsButtonVariant.Outlined" Icon="refresh" OnClick="LoadSettingsAsync">Retry</OdsButton>
        </div>
    }
    else if (VisibleSections.Count > 0)
    {
        @* One card per GROUP holding a grid of OdsSettingFields — not one card per setting. At 40+
           settings that was a stack of near-identical shells whose visual weight said nothing about
           the content; the grouping now does the work the 40 card borders were doing. *@
        <div class="ss-sect-stack">
            @foreach (var sec in VisibleSections)
            {
                var roundTripError = RoundTripError(sec);
                var dirtyHere = sec.Items.Count(IsDirty);
                var secretsHere = SecretsIn(sec);
                @* The badge counts the FIELDS the card renders, credentials included — a card showing
                   eight fields under a "5 settings" badge reads as a broken filter. The unsaved badge
                   above stays secrets-exempt on purpose: a credential writes on its own button and
                   never contributes to the page's Save. *@
                var fieldsHere = sec.Items.Count + secretsHere.Count;
                <section class="ss-sect" aria-labelledby="@($"ss-grp-{sec.Group}")">
                    <header class="ss-sect-h">
                        <span class="ss-sect-ic"><MudIcon Icon="@sec.Icon" Size="Size.Small" /></span>
                        <MudText Typo="Typo.h3" Class="ss-sect-t" id="@($"ss-grp-{sec.Group}")">@sec.Group</MudText>
                        @if (dirtyHere > 0)
                        {
                            <span class="ss-sect-badge">@dirtyHere unsaved</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <span class="ss-sect-count">@fieldsHere @(fieldsHere == 1 ? "setting" : "settings")</span>
                        }
                    </header>
                    @if (GroupPartial(sec))
                    {
                        <div class="ss-sect-band" role="note">
                            <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">info</span>
                            <div>Some settings in this group require additional permissions.</div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    @if (roundTripError is not null)
                    {
                        @* Group level, never on the export row: in the documented flow that row is
                           disabled (unlimited is on) and so unfocusable. *@
                        <div class="ss-sect-band error" id="@RoundTripAlertId(sec.Group)" role="alert" tabindex="-1">
                            <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">error_outline</span>
                            <div>@roundTripError</div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    <div class="ss-sect-body">
                        <div class="odc-sfield-grid">
                            @foreach (var item in sec.Items)
                            {
                                @SettingField(item)
                            }
                            @* Credentials sit in the SUBJECT card, in the same grid as the plaintext
                               settings around them — the API key beside the destination it is sent to,
                               the relay pair beside the from address it authenticates. They are last
                               within the card: an administrator reads what a feature does before what
                               authenticates it, and a write-only field is a poor thing to open on. *@
                            @foreach (var item in secretsHere)
                            {
                                var status = StatusFor(item);
                                <OdsSecretSettingField @key="item.Key"
                                                       AnchorId="@SecretAnchorId(item.Key)"
                                                       SecretKey="@item.Key"
                                                       Title="@item.Title"
                                                       Description="@item.Description"
                                                       State="@status.State"
                                                       UpdatedAt="@status.UpdatedAt"
                                                       UpdatedByDisplayName="@status.UpdatedByDisplayName"
                                                       IsDerivationKey="@item.IsDerivationKey"
                                                       Consequence="@item.Consequence"
                                                       Affects="@item.Affects"
                                                       Disabled="@(_phase != Phase.Ready || _isSaving)"
                                                       OnSave="@(value => SecretSettings.SetAsync(item.Key, value))"
                                                       OnClear="@(() => SecretSettings.ClearAsync(item.Key))"
                                                       OnChanged="OnSecretChangedAsync"
                                                       OnAnnounce="Announce" />
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>
            }

        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <MudCard Elevation="1" Class="ods-outline">
            <MudCardContent>
                <OdsEmptyState Icon="search_off" MutedIcon="true">
                    <Title>No settings match</Title>
                    <Description>Nothing here matches "@_search". Clear the search to see every setting.</Description>
                </OdsEmptyState>
            </MudCardContent>
        </MudCard>
    }

    @* The destructive gate in front of Save (issue #8 §3). It intercepts the page's single batch
       save when the SMTP host has changed to a different non-empty value, or STARTTLS has been turned
       off — both of which clear the stored relay credential on the server, in the same transaction as
       the settings write.

       Rendered unconditionally rather than inside an @if: the dialog owns its own visibility through
       Open, and mounting it only when the trigger appears would move focus into a component that was
       not in the DOM when the reader pressed Save.

       Confirm submits the WHOLE batch, exactly as an unguarded Save would; Cancel and Escape discard
       nothing at all. Focus returns to the row that caused it in every case — neither OdsModal nor
       OdsFormDialog restores it, so the page does. *@
    <OdsSecretClearOnSaveDialog Open="_clearDialogOpen"
                                OpenChanged="OnClearDialogOpenChanged"
                                Reason="_clearReason"
                                FromHost="@SavedSmtpHost"
                                ToHost="@DraftSmtpHost"
                                Secrets="@RelayCredentialTitles"
                                ReEnterAt="Credentials"
                                PendingCount="DirtyCount"
                                OnConfirm="ConfirmClearAsync" />

    @* Polite live region — save success / load failure announcements (WCAG 2.2 §4.1.3). *@
    <OdsLiveAnnouncer Message="@_announce" />
</MudStack>

@code {
    // ── One setting as an OdsSettingField ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // A switch or an export action has no text value to notch a label into, so it takes the sibling
    // tile shape and spans the grid; a text value is content-width and takes `Wide`; everything else
    // is a half-width notched frame.
    //
    // Inside the frame the control is just its value: the frame owns the label, and the helper line
    // owns the description, the range and the provenance — so nothing is passed for Label or Help on
    // the control itself. The MESSAGE likewise renders once, on the field; the control is handed a
    // non-empty placeholder error purely to flip `aria-invalid` and the error tint, since the sheet
    // hides the control's own help/error node inside the frame.
    private RenderFragment SettingField(SettingItem item) => item.Control switch
    {
        SettingControl.Toggle or SettingControl.Export => TileField(item),
        _ => FrameField(item),
    };

    @* @key, because a filter change otherwise re-matches fields POSITIONALLY — harmless only while
       draft state lives in the PAGE's key-indexed dictionaries (issue #444 §3). *@
    private RenderFragment FrameField(SettingItem item) => @<OdsSettingField
        @key="item.Key"
        Label="@item.Title"
        LabelId="@TitleId(item.Key)"
        HtmlFor="@(HasFocusableControl(item) ? ControlId(item.Key) : null)"
        HelpId="@DescId(item.Key)"
        Help="@HelpLine(item)"
        ErrorId="@ErrorId(item.Key)"
        Error="@ErrorFor(item)"
        AdvisoryId="@AdvisoryId(item.Key)"
        Advisory="@AdvisoryFor(item)"
        Bound="@BoundFor(item)"
        Dirty="@IsDirty(item)"
        Wide="@(item.Control == SettingControl.Text)"
        Class="@FieldClass(item)"
        data-ss-row="@item.Key">
        @RenderControl(item)
    </OdsSettingField>;

    private RenderFragment TileField(SettingItem item) => @<OdsSettingField
        @key="item.Key"
        Tile="true"
        Label="@item.Title"
        LabelId="@TitleId(item.Key)"
        HelpId="@DescId(item.Key)"
        Help="@HelpLine(item)"
        AdvisoryId="@AdvisoryId(item.Key)"
        Advisory="@AdvisoryFor(item)"
        Dirty="@IsDirty(item)"
        Class="@FieldClass(item)"
        data-ss-row="@item.Key">
        @RenderControl(item)
    </OdsSettingField>;

    // AriaLabel, not AriaLabelledBy: OdsSwitch names itself through MudSwitch's own AriaLabel, which
    // lands on the underlying input. A splatted aria-labelledby/aria-describedby would not — it would
    // reach MudSwitch as an unmatched parameter and be emitted as a meaningless DOM attribute.
    private RenderFragment ToggleControl(SettingItem item) => @<OdsSwitch
        Checked="@GetBool(item.Key)"
        CheckedChanged="@(v => SetBool(item.Key, v))"
        Disabled="@IsRowDisabled(item)"
        AriaLabel="@item.Title"
        AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy(item)" />;

    private RenderFragment NumberControl(SettingItem item) => @<OdsNumberField
        Id="@ControlId(item.Key)"
        Value="@((decimal?)GetInt(item.Key))"
        ValueChanged="@(v => SetInt(item.Key, v))"
        Min="@MinFor(item)" Max="@MaxFor(item)" Step="1" Align="right"
        Unit="@item.Unit"
        Disabled="@IsRowDisabled(item)"
        Error="@FieldStateFor(item)"
        AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy(item)" />;

    // The import/export size caps, in megabytes. The unit is pinned inside the input's trailing edge
    // rather than written as helper text (issue #343 §3 originally used Help="MB"): the helper slot
    // is where the error message goes, so a unit living there disappears exactly when the value is
    // wrong and the reader most needs to know what they are typing.
    private RenderFragment SizeControl(SettingItem item) => @<OdsNumberField
        Id="@ControlId(item.Key)"
        Value="@((decimal?)GetInt(item.Key))"
        ValueChanged="@(v => SetInt(item.Key, v))"
        Min="@MinFor(item)" Max="@MaxFor(item)" Step="1" Align="right"
        Unit="MB"
        Disabled="@IsRowDisabled(item)"
        Error="@FieldStateFor(item)"
        AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy(item)" />;

    // Inline, not stacked: there is no room inside the notched frame for a switch plus its own
    // label, and no second line to put them on. The pill carries the inverse action instead.
    private RenderFragment CapacityControl(SettingItem item)
    {
        var cap = Capacity(item.Key);
        return @<OdsCapacityField
            Variant="OdsCapacityFieldVariant.Inline"
            Value="@((decimal?)cap.Value)"
            ValueChanged="@(v => SetCapacityValue(item.Key, v))"
            Unlimited="@cap.Unlimited"
            UnlimitedChanged="@(on => SetCapacityUnlimited(item.Key, on, item.Title))"
            Label="@item.Title" Min="@MinFor(item)" Max="@MaxFor(item)"
            Disabled="@IsRowDisabled(item)"
            Error="@FieldStateFor(item)"
            AriaLabelledBy="@TitleId(item.Key)"
            AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy(item)" />;
    }

    // A text value's width is set by its content — a privacy-notice URL is ~39 characters — so its
    // field spans both grid columns rather than being squeezed into a half-width block.
    private RenderFragment TextControl(SettingItem item) => @<OdsTextInputField
        Id="@ControlId(item.Key)"
        Value="@GetText(item.Key)"
        ValueChanged="@(v => SetText(item.Key, v))"
        MaxLength="@(item.Max > 0 ? item.Max : null)"
        InputMode="@item.InputMode"
        Placeholder="@(item.Placeholder ?? "—")"
        Disabled="@IsRowDisabled(item)"
        Error="@FieldStateFor(item)"
        AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy(item)" />;

    // OdsNumberField already emits decimal? with no cast, so no ClampToInt analogue is needed here —
    // the #403 checked-(int) overflow simply cannot arise on this path. What a decimal row does need
    // is decimal BOUNDS, which SettingItem carries separately from the int Min/Max.
    private RenderFragment DecimalControl(SettingItem item) => @<OdsNumberField
        Id="@ControlId(item.Key)"
        Value="@GetDecimal(item.Key)"
        ValueChanged="@(v => SetDecimal(item.Key, v))"
        Min="@((double)item.DecimalMin)" Max="@((double)item.DecimalMax)" Step="0.01" Align="right"
        Unit="@item.Unit"
        Disabled="@IsRowDisabled(item)"
        Error="@FieldStateFor(item)"
        AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy(item)" />;

    // A stored 0.0-1.0 fraction, entered as a WHOLE PERCENT. The unit sits inside the input's
    // trailing edge, so it survives the helper slot being taken over by an error message.
    private RenderFragment PercentControl(SettingItem item) => @<OdsNumberField
        Id="@ControlId(item.Key)"
        Value="@GetPercent(item.Key)"
        ValueChanged="@(v => SetPercent(item.Key, v))"
        Min="@PercentOf(item.DecimalMin)" Max="@PercentOf(item.DecimalMax)" Step="1" Align="right"
        Unit="%"
        Disabled="@IsRowDisabled(item)"
        Error="@FieldStateFor(item)"
        AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy(item)" />;

    private RenderFragment ExportControl() => @<OdsButton
        Icon="file_download"
        Loading="_isExporting"
        OnClick="ExportDatabaseJson">
        Export database JSON
    </OdsButton>;
}
